<Base title="EU readiness" description="How Evidentia maps to the EU AI Act, GDPR, copyright, Cyber Resilience Act, NIS2, ePrivacy, DSA, accessibility and Data Act: applicability verdicts, implemented controls, evidence produced, customer responsibilities and items that require legal assessment. No compliance badges." path="/eu-readiness" pageType="TechArticle" breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'EU readiness', path: '/eu-readiness' }]}>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">EU readiness</span>
    <h1>Controls, evidence and responsibilities — per framework, without badges.</h1>
    <p class="lede">We researched each framework against primary sources (EUR-Lex, the Commission's AI Office, EDPB, ENISA, the Belgian DPA) and recorded an applicability verdict, the controls Evidentia implements, what remains the customer's responsibility, and what still needs legal assessment. The full analysis with dated citations is in the documentation.</p>
    <div class="callout warn"><strong>What we do not claim.</strong> Evidentia is not "EU compliant", "AI Act certified" or "GDPR approved" — no such certifications exist for this product category, and compliance is an outcome of how an organisation deploys and operates the system. This page is engineering information, not legal advice.</div>
  </section>
  <section class="block"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0"><table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Framework</th><th scope="col">Verdict</th><th scope="col">Why</th><th scope="col">Evidentia controls &amp; evidence</th><th scope="col">Customer responsibilities</th><th scope="col">Requires legal assessment</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{rows.map((r) => <tr>{r.map((c) => <td>{c}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="kicker">Verdict vocabulary: applicable · potentially applicable · customer-specific · non-applicable · not yet applicable · requires legal assessment. Hard dates recorded in <a href="/docs/eu-governance">docs/EU_GOVERNANCE.md</a>; 68 primary sources in <code>governance/sources.yaml</code>.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="block"><div class="wrap prose">
    <h2>Article 50 in practice</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Marking (Art. 50(2), provider duty).</strong> Every published page carries a signed manifest bound to the content hash, IPTC <code>digitalSourceType</code>, meta tags and a CreativeWork JSON-LD block. Verification uses the published Ed25519 public key.</li>
      <li><strong>Disclosure (Art. 50(4), deployer duty).</strong> AI-assisted pages render a visible "AI transparency notice" naming the accountable reviewer. Policies can require it for every publication.</li>
      <li><strong>Human-review route.</strong> The Commission's guidelines expect substantive fact-checking, named editorial responsibility and no AI edits after sign-off. Evidentia's verification, four-eyes approval bound to the body hash, and post-approval lock implement exactly that — and the ledger proves it.</li>
      <li><strong>Limits.</strong> Token-level watermarking is a model-provider capability; Evidentia records which model produced a draft and signs the page, but does not watermark text itself.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>Dated watchlist</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>2 Dec 2026 — end of the Art. 50 grace period for generative systems already on the market before 2 Aug 2026.</li>
      <li>12 Jan 2027 — Data Act: cloud switching charges must be zero.</li>
      <li>2 Feb 2027 — Transparency Code of Practice interoperability signpost.</li>
      <li>11 Dec 2027 — CRA essential requirements fully applicable; 2 Dec 2027 — AI Act Annex III high-risk obligations.</li>
    </ul>
  </div></section>
</Base>
